t in a D+3 district during a favorable environment, combined with Powell's clear money advantage and unanimous expert calls for a flip, points toward Democrats; the lone poll is narrower but too small to outweigh the rest.", "watch": "A strong Harding debate performance or a late Republican outside-spending surge on affordability could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 2nd District is an open seat after Republican Don Bacon's retirement, in a district that leans Democratic by about three points. The national political environment favors Democrats this year, and Denise Powell holds the money advantage: she has raised more than Brinker Harding and benefits from roughly $3 million in outside support, while Harding has drawn almost none. The Omaha mayor's endorsement and a focus on affordability have shaped the campaign's closing weeks.\n\nEvery major rating outfit calls the seat a Democratic flip, ranging from tilt to safe, and prediction markets put Democratic odds above 90 percent. The only public poll, a small September survey, showed Powell up four, narrower than the broader picture suggests but still ahead. With one debate remaining in mid-October, the seat leans clearly toward a Democratic pickup, though the margin depends on turnout in Omaha's suburbs.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.914, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9146, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8905, "margin": 8.5}]}